{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_uk2oiqdhmj2eszsnotaa6dn3zwwtq3sn3adt27b2hwqqnjbsry3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **70/70 tests pass, 0 warnings, symbol diff empty, all 60 table rows checked off.**\n\n## One real bug found and fixed\n\n`src/lib.rs` used `std::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping`, which carries a **debug-only null-pointer precondition check**. So `driver(NULL, len>0)` aborted with `SIGABRT` in the debug build where the C segfaults (`SIGSEGV`) — release matched only by accident. Fixed by calling libc `memcpy`, literally the function `driver.c` calls (which also removed a `if n > 0` guard the C doesn't have). Identical in every profile now.\n\n## Also fixed: the suite was silently vacuous\n\nThe crate's only `crate-type` is `cdylib`, so **`cargo test` never re-emits `libdriver.so`**. All 10 mutants I injected into `src/lib.rs` initially \"passed\" the whole suite against a stale `.so`. Added `assert_so_fresh` (harness refuses to run if the `.so` is older than any `src/*.rs`), and `run_all.sh` always builds first. Re-run after the fix: **10 killed, 0 survived**.\n\n## Artifacts (`translation/`)\n\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 2 C symbols (`driver`, `fma_array`); Rust diff empty; `inner` correctly private in both |\n| `ERRORS.md` | **16 rows, all ✔** — C has *zero* explicit error checks (greps for `return`/`assert`/`NULL`/`if` all empty), so rows are derived from the loop guards, the VLA, the `len*sizeof(int)` → `size_t` conversion, and the overflow-capable `a*b+c` |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | **44 rows, all ✔** — 9 pointer-aliasing configs × 10 value shapes × 19 lengths, driving the low-level `fma_array` directly, not just the `driver` wrapper |\n| `VERIFICATION.md` | completion gate + divergence log |\n| `run_all.sh`, `mutation_check.sh` | automation |\n\n## Coverage notes\n\n- **stdout is compared at the fd level**, byte-for-byte (the library's only output is `printf`). Required forcing `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` — concurrent libtest progress lines were landing inside captures.\n- Ran under: `debug` + `release` (`panic=\"abort\"`), `--all-features` + `--no-default-features` (crate declares none), and against an extra **`-O2` C build** to confirm the wrapping-overflow ground truth isn't an `-O0` artifact.\n- Trap rows run **out of process** and compare exact signals (`SIGSEGV` vs `SIGSEGV`), not just \"both failed\".\n\n## Two deliberate, documented divergences (UB inputs)\n\n`driver` with **negative `len`** (negative-size VLA + `memcpy` of ~2^64 bytes) and with **`len` past the stack limit** (measured: 2,000,000 OK / 2,100,000 faults at 8 MiB) both kill the C process. Rust clamps / heap-allocates and survives. Not reproduced on purpose — a UB crash isn't stable across compiler, `-O` level or `ulimit -s`. Both rows still have tests asserting (a) the C *does* trap, so the divergence re-flags if the C build changes, and (b) Rust emits **no** stdout, so the two never produce *differing* bytes. Everything in the defined domain is byte-identical."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":328,"cache_read_input_tokens":166222,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":328,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37ad3814-120d-4cd3-86a4-b047164fbfba","uuid":"a45ad4fc-4acc-4945-ad85-aaff16d3cbc6","timestamp":"2026-09-03T11:48:49.842Z"}
